ct of monetary  policy are you deal with is the how  Indian monetary policy coped with  volatile capital flows and once again  this is topical because well the most  interesting is in the current crisis in  the West is that there were huge capital  flows between europe and the u.s. in  particular in both directions and how  they also had a role in causing sort of  problems that exist in our case in the  case of emerging markets it's not just  india but other emerging markets in asia  and latin america a coupe with large  volatile volatility and capital flows  and we had to then adapt our fiscal and  monetary policies and using a host of  different instruments including capital  account management techniques to be able  to have an independent monetary policy  while having some capricorn management  some capital controls and along with  along with development of financial  markets and the flexible exchange rate  and we are not that different in a way  from the other emerging markets and one  of the interesting things about the  current crisis really is that europe in  the u.s. in great difficulty while  emerging market countries in asia and  latin america have not had financial  sector problems as a consequence of this  of this crisis and india's among those  countries and so the third part of the  book basically deals with that and  finally there is a whole section of the  book on the global financial crisis and  the trying to document how it was  somewhat loose or accommodative monetary  policy in the early 2000s after the.com  crash combined with very light or light  touch regulation which basically took  the regulator's eyes off what is really  happening in the economy and then we had  all the problems of subprime and then of  course everything else that happened  including a huge explosion of financial  innovations and derivative  that contributed to the crisis okay so  we'll actually talk about each of those  pieces what I'm wondering is how how  your perspective differs on India's  economic growth in comparison to what is  widely believed about its economy well  i'm not sure if it does differ but  nonetheless i suppose that one thing  that I would say is that there is a  perception that we we are very poor  economic performance in the first 30  years he is 1950 1980 and then much  better one now that is really true in  terms of growth rates that the first 30  years we are but three three and a half  percent growth in gdp for over 30 a  period and then average of what six six  and a half percent last 30 years and of  course something like eight percent the  last seventy eight percent last ten  years so it's been going continuously so  one of the points that I do make is that  this has been a consistent story and  essentially going from low savings and  investment rates to higher savings  investment rate so it's been a  consistent growth over the decades in  domestic savings and investment while of  course in the earlier period the corner  is much more closed and the letter P  requires much more open but a consistent  story between different policy regimes  has been this this growth in savings and  investment and that is of course what is  necessary to achieve economic growth and  the other point is that even the first  30 years was a big departure or the  previous fifty to a hundred years where  we had zero growth so that three three  and a half percent was very low by a  current standard but nonetheless it was  a big departure from the previous 50  years okay what are some of the current  challenges India faces well the  immediate challenge of course just now  emerged really in since last six months  to one year that first after the 2008-9  crisis in the  world we were able to cope with that  quite well so that we also did a fiscal  stimulus like many countries did we did  the monetary stimulus as well and that's  how you coped with it that's what I hope  with it primarily and we were able to do  to restrict the impact on growth so that  in the first year that is 2008-9 it  growth just went down to what six point  eight percent and then came back up to  over eight percent in 2009-10 and 10 11  it's now that I think I think most  observers are expected that the world  would be coming out of the crisis in  2011-2012 but because of the eurozone  crisis now and also some of the problems  right it states to do with the fiscal  situation and some breakdown in Congress  in terms of the agreement and what to do  that we are now on the brink of another  recession in the developed economies so  as a consequence of that the immediate  challenge of course is to cope with what  may happen given the uncertainty in the  rest of the world okay and less  immediate challenge okay and how do you  work to sustain long-term growth what  are some of the strategies you think  would work well the some of the issues  that are emerging in India are much more  to do with human resource development  one big opportunity we have is that our  demographic situation is different from  other countries in the sense that over  the next 20 to 30 years the proportion  of people in the working age group say  between 20 and 50 would be much higher  than the other groups that is children  and old people so this is what people  characterize a demographic dividend that  will have that many more people working  so it's a greater opportunity to have  much higher growth also with more people  working with lower with fewer dependence  that you can have higher savings and  there  and consumption and and higher  investment so this is really should be  here at a very high growth however a  problem really is that we the stock of  education the stock of skills is low  even relative to some of our peers okay  and so a major challenge is to one make  sure that that the children now get good  health good nutrition and good education  for the future but also that people who  are already in the labor force  particularly say in the age of 